\subsection{Auditing an Index Entry That Names More Than One Kind of Thing}

An index mixes languages, implementations, tools, and published
specifications. A reference to an implementation is not necessarily
a reference to its language definition, and two systems can have
similar names without being aliases. Use stable index identities
when recording coverage. An editor can then distinguish a stale
chapter reference from a chapter that merely mentions a related
entry.

\textbf{Contract.} Given an index entry with a canonical identity,
a set of chapter anchors, and a reviewed coverage ledger, return
anchors that lack support or no longer resolve. The ledger assigns
canonical identities to substantive passages, not to every textual
mention. Display aliases map to one canonical identity only after
review; an ambiguous search term must prompt selection rather than
merge distinct entries. Printed chapter numbers come from resolved
anchors.

\begin{sudocode}[Check reviewed coverage anchors]
\Let{id}{\Call{ResolveDisplayName}{\mathrm{entry}}}
\If{$id$ is missing or ambiguous}
  \State \Return resolve entry identity first
\EndIf
\Let{D}{\text{empty findings list}}
\For{each declared anchor $a$}
  \To{Resolve labels, not chapter numbers copied by hand.}
  \If{$a$ no longer resolves}
    \State append $(a,\mathrm{stale})$ to $D$
  \ElsIf{$id$ is absent from $\mathrm{Coverage}[a]$}
    \To{A related system does not satisfy this entry.}
    \State append $(a,\mathrm{needsReview})$ to $D$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return $D$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Suppose a draft index entry for the Haskell
2010 Report points to one label in Chapter \(121\) and one in
Chapter \(128\). In a toy ledger the first label is tagged
with the report's identity, while the second points to the
STG discussion and is tagged only with GHC. Both labels resolve,
but the audit returns one needs-review finding for the second.
Resolving to a real chapter is not enough.

\Cref{fig:visual-130} highlights the second check: a live anchor must
also have evidence for the canonical item being indexed.

% Explanatory visual
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[tome diagram,
    check/.style={tome box,minimum width=4.75cm,minimum height=1.15cm}]
  \node[tome box dark] (entry) at (0,0) {Index entry: Haskell 2010 Report};
  \node[check] (first) at (-2.9,-1.6) {Chapter 121 anchor\\Resolves};
  \node[check] (second) at (2.9,-1.6) {Chapter 128 anchor\\Resolves};
  \node[check,fill=TomeBgAlt] (match) at (-2.9,-3.2)
    {\textbf{Keep}\\Ledger identifies the Report};
  \node[check,dashed,draw=TomeGold] (review) at (2.9,-3.2)
    {\textbf{Review}\\Ledger identifies only GHC};
  \draw[tome arrow] (entry.south) -- (first.north);
  \draw[tome arrow] (entry.south) -- (second.north);
  \draw[tome arrow] (first) -- (match);
  \draw[tome arrow] (second) -- (review);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{A toy coverage audit: both links work, but the GHC passage
does not substantiate the entry for the language report.}
\label{fig:visual-130}
\end{figure}

Each passing anchor has a matching coverage record for the same
canonical item, regardless of how that item is spelled in prose.
The check is linear in claimed anchors with indexed lookups.
It does not find missing entries: perform a reverse pass over
the ledger to find documented topics with no index reference.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:lang-index-summary}

This index has catalogued the programming languages, compiler implementations, tools, frameworks, and standards discussed throughout this book. The coverage spans:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Over 50 programming languages, from historical (Fortran, Lisp) to cutting-edge (Rust, Lean).
  \item Over 30 compiler and interpreter implementations, from production systems (LLVM, GCC, GHC) to research prototypes (Mix, LMS).
  \item Over 20 tools and frameworks for parsing, language workbenches, proof assistants, and code generation.
  \item Major standards and specifications that define the languages we use.
\end{itemize}

The reader can use this index to:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Find where a specific language or tool is discussed in the book.
  \item Identify related languages and tools within the same family.
  \item Understand the scope of the book's coverage.
  \item Find starting points for further exploration of specific topics.
\end{enumerate}

The diversity of languages and tools reflects the richness of the field of programming language development. Each language embodies a set of design decisions; each tool embodies a set of engineering choices. By studying this diversity, we gain insight into the space of possible designs and the trade-offs that shape them.
